
  First, what I found exploring the repo — it changes the shape of this conversation:

  index.html   (11 lines: <h1>Tasks</h1> and an empty <main>)

  That's the entire project. One commit, "initial: empty tasks page." There is no task model, no users, no storage, no
  server, no build tooling, no tests.

  That matters because your request has three things nested inside it, and only the outermost one is "notifications":

  1. [1mTasks[0m — there is no task entity. Nothing to change, so nothing to notify about.
  2. [1mUsers[0m — "users get notified" implies identity and per-user state. There's no concept of a user, and a static HTML
     file has no way to tell two people apart.
  3. [1m"Tasks they care about"[0m — a subscription/interest relationship between a user and a task. That's its own model, and
     it's the actual heart of your request.

  So I can't design a notifications system yet — it would be a spec written entirely against imaginary interfaces, and
  every guess I made about the task model would become a constraint you'd have to either accept or pay to undo. The
  decomposition question comes before the design questions.

  Let me start with the one that determines everything downstream.
